pe":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Why cash:** In-kind baskets work only for deliverable assets. Many derivatives (swaps, futures, OTC options) are bilateral contracts or exchange positions tied to the fund's own accounts, margin, and ISDA/clearing arrangements, so they usually can't be handed to an AP. On creations, the fund takes cash and opens or extends positions with counterparties. On redemptions, it closes positions and pays cash. The cash component, or transaction fees, can shift those trading costs to the transacting AP.\n\n**Contrast:** Transferable securities, such as listed equities and many bonds, can move in-kind. This avoids fund-level trading and may let the fund deliver low-basis lots for tax efficiency under §852(b)(6).\n\n**Limits:** This is a general rationale, not any specific fund's practice. Actual baskets follow each fund's prospectus, Rule 6c-11 basket policies, and AP agreements. Custom or mixed baskets, operational factors, and tax or cost tradeoffs vary, so verify with fund documents.","ttft_ms":1697,"type":"result","duration_ms":3998,"uuid":"74c4fc47-0dba-421f-9cc8-1348b7310e1e","ttft_stream_ms":1149,"time_to_request_ms":66,"first_content_frame_ms":1149,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
